---
import SectionHead from "./SectionHead.astro";
import { workedExample } from "@/lib/worked-example";

const verdicts = [
  {
    id: "claim",
    label: "A breakthrough claim",
    evidence: "A press release, or a video of a sample levitating.",
    verdict: "Does not qualify",
    qualifies: false,
  },
  {
    id: "demonstration",
    label: "An independent demonstration",
    evidence: `An original experimental report plus a replication by a separate institution with no shared authors and independently prepared samples. Both publish methods, measurements and uncertainty before ${workedExample.cutoff.date}.`,
    verdict: "Qualifies as evidence",
    qualifies: true,
  },
];
---

<!-- 02 · Why resolution matters. -->
<section id="why" class="home-band home-band--paper home-band--dark-raised" aria-labelledby="why-title">
  <div class="site-frame">
    <SectionHead index="02" label="Why resolution matters" title="Who decides what actually happened?" headingId="why-title">
      <p>
        A prediction market can settle itself, but it cannot see the world. Before it pays anyone, something has to
        establish what occurred. When someone stands to profit from the wrong answer, that something becomes the
        target.
      </p>
    </SectionHead>

    <div class="home-why">
      <div class="home-why__prose">
        <p>
          For small markets this is unremarkable: a team checks the evidence, a committee decides, token holders vote.
          As the stakes grow, manipulating the resolution can pay better than trading on the correct outcome, and every
          ambiguity in the question becomes a point of attack.
        </p>
        <p>
          Augur was built on the position that no company or committee should hold the final word. Disputed outcomes are
          settled through an open economic process instead: participants stake value on competing answers and lose it
          when they back the wrong one.
        </p>
        <p class="home-pull">What happens when participants disagree about that answer?</p>
      </div>

      <div class="home-why__evidence">
        <p class="home-label augur-type-editorial-label">
          <span>Worked example</span>
          <span class="home-label__aside">What counts as evidence</span>
        </p>
        <div class="home-verdicts">
          {verdicts.map(({ id, label, evidence, verdict, qualifies }) => (
            <article class="home-verdict" data-qualifies={qualifies ? "" : undefined} aria-labelledby={`verdict-${id}`}>
              <h3 id={`verdict-${id}`} class="home-verdict__label augur-type-editorial-label">{label}</h3>
              <p class="home-verdict__evidence">{evidence}</p>
              <p class="home-verdict__result">
                <span class="home-marker" data-shape={qualifies ? "filled" : "open"} aria-hidden="true"></span>
                <span class="home-verdict__key augur-type-editorial-label">Verdict</span>
                <strong>{verdict}</strong>
              </p>
            </article>
          ))}
        </div>
        <p class="home-caption">The question has to be precise enough to tell these apart.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
